{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Mul","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.gram_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.gram_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GramChiralAntiShare.lean#L147-L153","doc":"The Gram norm itself is **positive**, not merely nonzero, once any block\ncontributes. Recorded because the probe's framing (an indefinite Frobenius\nGram matrix) would not lead one to expect it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.chiral_anti_share_eq_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.chiral_anti_share_eq_half","kind":"theorem","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GramChiralAntiShare.lean#L127-L145","doc":"**Main result.** With every contribution non-negative and the conjugate of\neach contributing block vanishing (the two-row cap), the chiral-antisymmetric\nshare of the Gram norm is exactly `1/2`.\n\n`anti` is the probe's `R_anti`: half the ℓ¹ distance between a block and its\nbar-image, summed over chiral pairs. The hypothesis `hconj` is the vanishing\nof the conjugate block, so each pair contributes `|c_λ − 0|/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.sum_abs_eq_sum_of_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.sum_abs_eq_sum_of_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GramChiralAntiShare.lean#L119-L125","doc":"**The sign fact the share needs.** When every contribution is\nnon-negative, the ℓ¹ total coincides with the signed total. This is precisely\nwhere an indefinite pairing would have broken the argument. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.gramContrib_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.gramContrib_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GramChiralAntiShare.lean#L109-L117","doc":"A per-block Gram contribution `c_λ = y_λ · tr(ρ(W) ρ(W^♯))` is non-negative\nwhenever the Wedderburn weight is. Combining the two lemmas above: the trace\nfactor is a Frobenius square because `♯` is transposition on symmetric\ngenerators. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.trace_mul_transpose_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.trace_mul_transpose_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GramChiralAntiShare.lean#L100-L107","doc":"`tr(M Mᵀ)` is the sum of the squares of `M`'s entries, hence non-negative.\nThis is the Frobenius norm squared. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.reverse_prod_eq_transpose_of_symmetric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.GramChiralAntiShare.reverse_prod_eq_transpose_of_symmetric","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/GramChiralAntiShare.lean#L80-L98","doc":"**The structural step.** If every letter of a word is a symmetric matrix,\nthe reversed product is the transpose of the forward product.\n\nThis is what turns the reversal anti-involution `♯` into matrix transposition,\nand hence what makes the Gram contribution a sum of squares rather than an\narbitrary value of an indefinite pairing. ","signatureChunk":0}]}